<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorch am Rhein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorch am Rhein is a small wine town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at the mouth of the Wisper, in the Upper Middle Rhine Valley. From 1919 to 1923 it was the administrative centre of the Free State Bottleneck, a strip of unoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> cut off between the French and American bridgeheads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorch-am-rhein.jpg" alt="Lorch am Rhein" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Traveler100 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorch-rs-01s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lorch am Rhein is a small town in the Rheingau-Taunus-Kreis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and belongs to the Rhine Gorge World Heritage Site. It lies about 10 km north of the bend of the Rhine near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudesheim-am-rhein">Rüdesheim</a>, between Rüdesheim am Rhein and Sankt Goarshausen, and owes its setting to its position at the mouth of the Wisper and to its steep vineyards. Its municipal area runs into the wooded Wisper valley, and the Rheinsteig hiking trail follows the heights above it. The Rhine island and nature reserve called Lorcher Werth lies nearby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was settled early by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a> and later the Mattiaci. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> advanced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> in the first century CE, followed by the Alamanni and, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. The town's oldest documentary mention is from 1085, when Archbishop Wezilo recorded a donation to the cathedral chapter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> that included a house and vineyards in Lorch. In the Middle Ages Lorch was the northern bastion of the Archbishopric of Mainz facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rheingau">Rheingau</a> and, from the twelfth century, stood at the southern end of the Rheingauer Gebück, a border defence of stunted trees. A parish was first documented in 1254. There was warfare in Lorch in 1460, 1631 and 1794.</p><p>On 10 January 1919 the Free State of Bottleneck, a provisional state-like entity between the occupation zones after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, was proclaimed with Lorch as its de facto capital, and many coats of arms of the microstate still recall the time. In the early 1960s the Bundeswehr came with its Flugabwehrregiment 5, and a new settlement, the Ranselberg, was built for soldiers and their families. The barracks in the Wisper valley were closed in 1993, and an industrial park of about twenty firms later rose on the former military land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Winegrowing and tourism characterise the town. Its vineyards, among them the Schlossberg and the Kapellenberg, lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rheingau">Rheingau</a> region and are planted mainly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riesling">Riesling</a>. The Gothic Catholic parish church of St Martin was completed in the fourteenth century over a Late Romanesque basilica, and its high altar by Hans from Worms was installed in 1483. The Hilchenhaus of the mid sixteenth century is known as the loveliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> building on the Middle Rhine. The town's noble family named itself von Lorch, and its best-known member, Johann Hilchen (1484 to 1548), was a knight and Imperial field marshal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lorch am Rhein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The two circles left a narrow gap near <b>Lorch</b> and Kaub, cut off from unoccupied Germany by hills, which became known from January 1919 as the Free State Bottleneck.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-bottleneck">Free State Bottleneck</a></li></ul></section>
